|~ ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   qlang LSP showcase

   Open this file in VS Code with the qlang extension active
   to exercise the language server:
     1. Hover — the page of a verb, a path, a keyword
     2. Ctrl+Click — Go to Definition
     3. Shift+F12 — Find References
     4. Ctrl+Shift+O — Document Symbols
     5. Inside a call — Signature Help
     6. Ctrl+Space — Completion
     7. Typing — Diagnostics

   The file is one text: its declarations at its head, each ended
   by `;`, and one query after them, each example a group the one
   before it joins by `|`, its literal replacing the value in the
   pipe. A declaration adds a word: a verb, called by its name on a
   value, is declared under the label of the values it serves, and
   `::any` holds the verbs that mean the same for every value; a
   value of the subject is named by `with` for the code of one step.
   The rules it holds: [QSSLF#catalog]. ~|


|~| ── The declarations ──────────────────────────────────────
|~| Code declared under the label of the values it serves is a verb
|~| its name calls, and `~double` hands it on as code. Ctrl+Click
|~| double to jump to its declaration; Shift+F12 finds the
|~| declaration and its uses. A kind is built from `::kind` by
|~| commands, `wires` a field of its payload held to its kinds, and
|~| a value put under it keeps them. A verb declared under it serves
|~| its values alone, called by its name on the value, and is built
|~| from `::fn`, `takes` an input of the call held to its kinds. A
|~| doc stands in the slot of the declaration it documents.

:number/double |~~ Doubles a number. ~~| mul 2;

:Person |~~ A person, a name and an age. ~~| ::kind
  | accepts |~~ The name and the age. ~~| ::map
  | wires :name |~~ The name. ~~| /name ::string
  | wires :age |~~ The age in years. ~~| /age ::number;
:Person/adult |~~ Whether the person is of age. ~~| (age | gte 18);
:Person/greet |~~ The person's name after a greeting. ~~| ::fn
  | takes :greeting |~~ The words before the name. ~~| /0 ::string
  | impl ~(name | prepend greeting);

|~ a block comment between two declarations ~|
:any/z |~~ The doc in the slot of z. ~~| ~(1);


|~| ── 1. Hover ─────────────────────────────────────────────
|~| Hover count, /name and :age.

[1 2 3 4 5] | count

| ({:name "Ann" :age 30} | get /name)


|~| ── 2. Code ──────────────────────────────────────────────
|~| Code is written bare where a verb takes it: `apply` runs it on
|~| the value in the pipe, `map` on each element, `filter` to keep
|~| the elements it holds of.

| (5 | apply (add 1 | mul 2))

| ([1 2 3] | map (mul 2))

| ([1 2 3] | filter (gt 1))

| (5 | double)

| ([1 2 3] | map double)


|~| ── 3. A kind of your own ─────────────────────────────────

| (::Person{:name "Ann" :age 30} | adult)

| (::Person{:name "Ann" :age 30} | greet "hello ")

| (::Person{:name "Ann" :age 30} | greet 1 !| tag)

| (::Person{:name 7 :age 30} !| tag)

| ({:name "Bo" :age 15} | adult !| tag)


|~| ── 4. Two fields of one record ───────────────────────────
|~| A modifier is computed against the subject of its own step,
|~| so two fields meet in one step, or `with` names them for the
|~| code of one step.

| ({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty)

| ({:price 4 :qty 3} | with [/price /qty] (price | mul qty))


|~| ── 5. A fold ─────────────────────────────────────────────
|~| A verb written alone is called with the value so far as its
|~| subject and the element as its first arg; any other code runs
|~| on the pair of the value so far and the element.

| ([1 2 3] | reduce 0 add)

| ([{:x 1} {:x 2}] | reduce 0 (sumOf /0 (get /1 | get /x)))


|~| ── 6. Names and their scope ──────────────────────────────
|~| Code holds declarations of its own at its head, each ended by
|~| `;`: a name is declared once in its scope, and one declared
|~| inside parentheses closes with them; `with` names a value of the
|~| subject for the code of one step.

| apply (
    :x 1;
    :y (x | add 1);
    42 | with {:inner it} (inner | add y))


|~| ── 7. Signature Help and Completion ──────────────────────
|~| Inside a call of a verb of the language the active input
|~| follows the modifier under the cursor: condition, then,
|~| else. After `|` Ctrl+Space lists the verbs the kind of the
|~| value answers and the names the file declares.

| (5 | if (gt 0) ("pos") ("neg"))

| ("text" | upper)


|~| ── 8. Failures ───────────────────────────────────────────
|~| `|` passes a failure by and `!|` catches it; `doc`
|~| answers the page of what refused, which tells the repair.

| ("hello" | add 1 !| tag)

| ([1 "x" 3] | map (add 10 !| 0))

|~ a block comment between two steps ~|
| ("hello" | add 1 !| doc)
